<h2>The Paper Problem</h2>

<p>Fabric receipt, batch tracking, outside processing, gate passes, and issuance all create records that have to agree. When those records live across paper registers and separate files, finding the current state becomes slow and error-prone.</p>

<p>The goal was not to replace every human decision. It was to create a reliable path from a physical stock movement to a searchable operational record.</p>

<h2>Why ERPNext</h2>

<p>I evaluated a few options. SAP and Oracle were way out of budget. Custom-built systems would take months. ERPNext hit the sweet spot:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Open source</strong> with no license fees</li>
  <li><strong>Python-based</strong> so I could write custom scripts</li>
  <li><strong>Built-in modules</strong> for inventory, stock, and manufacturing</li>
  <li><strong>Web-based</strong> so anyone on the factory floor could access it from a browser</li>
</ul>

<h2>What I Customized</h2>

<p>ERPNext supplied the platform; my work was adapting it to the way this operation moves material. That distinction matters: this is an ERPNext/Frappe implementation and customization, not a claim that I wrote an ERP from scratch.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Stock movement</strong> aligned with receipt, transfer, and issuance events</li>
  <li><strong>Batch and LOT references</strong> that follow material through processing</li>
  <li><strong>Gate passes</strong> for controlled movement outside the facility</li>
  <li><strong>Critical Path fields and reports</strong> for production visibility</li>
  <li><strong>Scripts and barcode-assisted steps</strong> where they reduce repeated entry</li>
</ul>

<p>Changes were introduced incrementally so each workflow could be checked against the physical process before expanding the rollout.</p>

<h2>The Adoption Challenge</h2>

<p>Building the system was one thing. Getting a team of factory workers to actually use it was another. Most of them had never used anything beyond WhatsApp on their phones.</p>

<p>I handled this by walking through each role's specific workflow, reducing unnecessary decisions in the interface, and treating operator feedback as part of the implementation.</p>

<h2>What the System Improves</h2>

<p>The system creates a consistent operational trail between stock events, batch references, gate passes, and reports. It gives the team one structure for investigating discrepancies and reviewing work in progress.</p>

<p>Because the system is internal, this portfolio uses an architecture diagram rather than exposing production records or employee data.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>The hardest part of an ERP implementation isn't the code. It's getting people to trust the new system.</p>
</blockquote>

